 they wanted  to give us essentially nothing and so  we're moving on without them  i don't think that that's a waste of  time what  will become a waste of time is now  if they don't simply make this happen  via budget reconciliation  can they actually do that i don't know i  don't know that joe manchin  actually wants the bill the way that it  is and  by the way i mean from an infrastructure  perspective  west virginia is a disaster and by  infrastructure i mean  traditional infrastructure like bridges  and tunnels and roads and this type of  thing uh but  broadband infrastructure healthcare  infrastructure  educational infrastructure and on and on  if there is anybody in the senate who  should be desperate for the biggest  possible infrastructure package it  actually should be joe manchin  but as we talked about yesterday i  wasn't kidding when i said joe manchin  was going to be a problem  and it's not even clear to me that  democrats have the 50 votes  in order to pass this via budget  reconciliation if we step back for a  second  i've gotten emails from people saying is  this something that can be done via  budget reconciliation sometimes  you'll hear some on the left say  joe biden should just do medicare for  all through budget reconciliation first  of all joe biden doesn't support  medicare for all but putting that  aside my best reading of when budget  reconciliation can be used  would not allow it to be used for  medicare for all  that being said there is no question  that budget reconciliation  can be used to pass joe biden's  infrastructure bill  with 50 votes in the senate rather than  the 60 you would normally need  but that's only if you have all 50 votes  from democratic senators  and it's not at this point clear that  joe biden actually does  the new york times also reporting on the  stalled negotiations not really stalled  ended negotiations  saying after weeks of failed efforts to  bridge deep divides the president  pulled the plug turning to a bipartisan  group  to try to salvage a chance at compromise  my question is how long do you keep  going at it from this approach  because you end up with what are  sometimes these  pyrrhic victories where you do so much  damage in the process of negotiating  something that republicans will accept  that you are really compromising on your  ability to pass something that's  substantive  and with the current republicans as we  saw with the covet bill  sometimes you spend all this time  negotiating and they all vote against it  anyway so  my sense is that we're sort of getting  to the end here  uh the new york times writes sort of  pseudo-optimistically  the breakdown did not close off the  possibility of a bipartisan compromise  entirely and the white house signaled  that the president would continue  seeking one  he shifted his focus to a bipartisan  group of centrist senators  who have been working separately on an  alternative calling three of them  personally to cheer on efforts and  encourage them to work with top white  house officials to hammer out a deal but  even if the group can agree on a plan  palpable to joe biden  there's no guarantee enough republicans  would accept it  that that is exactly the point that uh  needs to be considered  how much longer are we doing this with  republicans who you know  it's conceivable that you get i don't  know  joe manchin and mitt romney in a room  and  they come up with something that they  say they would support  but then do you still have 60 votes  or are you put in the position of having  to go through budget reconciliation and  then you're just arguing with your own  side you're arguing with joe manchin joe  listen  we have 49 we need 50. what are you  willing to do for us and this is the  exact i mean  when you talk about the type of partisan  gridlock that sometimes it's bipartisan  gridlock when you talk about  the type of partisan gridlock where the  american people just look at it and say  this is why i am dis i i a